From 3ff69edf97abe91a3c803a7fb18fa15cc74f644d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 13:20:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] luma: a call looks like a call, and every live island
 leads with one tile
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A voice call in Claude Desktop drew as a calendar entry: a "SEP 16" date
tile, "Claude · 03:23" as an eyebrow and "11:40 AM" on the right. Luma
Calls published exactly what it should -- category "call", the call's
start, a mute that works and an end-call it cannot perform -- but the
island never read the category. Any publication with a start was dressed
as an event, so a call's answer time became a meeting date. Its controls
were dropped too, because "call.mute" had no glyph under that exact name.

liveActivity.js now decides the dress from the category, and the calendar
dress needs both category "event" and a start it can place. A call is the
kit's call: the application's icon (the running application Luma Calls
names, never a guess) or the caller's initials on an identity tint, a live
dot, "Call · 00:42" counted by the Shell every second, a microphone that is
green while live and ink-2 when muted, and red end-call -- each only when
the application really offers it. Anything else, including a category the
Shell does not know, is generic: the publisher's icon or the activity's
glyph, never a date. When several are live, a call outranks recording,
navigation, timers, transfers, installs, media and upcoming events.

Every leading tile -- call, timer, anything else, and media artwork -- is
now the dock icon's 36px at radius 10. Media artwork was masked at 9px and
fitted inside the square, so a 16:9 thumbnail drew short with its corners
on empty background; it is now cropped to fill the square (decoded no
larger than three tiles across) with the kit's light rim.
